      "message": "Yes, I\u0027d expect this function to return _something_ (instead of `()`).\n\nIMO, this would be a good use for a ZST:\n```\nstruct VcpufreqInfo {}\n\nstruct DeviceTreeInfo {\n    // ...\n    vcpufreq: Option\u003cVcpufreqInfo\u003e,\n}\n```\n\nWhere the option is used during patching (to address the other open comment):\n```\nlet node \u003d fdt.node_mut(\"/vcpufreq\")?.unwrap(); \nif vcpufreq.is_some() {\n    node.setprop_addrrange_inplace(cstr!(\"reg\"), addr, size)\n} else {\n    node.nop()\n}\n```\n\nYou could even parse the address and size into that struct, to avoid duplicating the logic that `size \u003d n_cpus * 8` and make the APIs and flow clearer (at the expense of a few bytes of stack) with the following functions (note the different error types):\n\n- `read_vcpufreq[_info](\u0026Fdt) -\u003e libfdt::Result\u003cVcpufreqInfo\u003e`\n- `validate_vcpufreq(\u0026VcpufreqInfo, num_cpus: usize) -\u003e Result\u003c(), FdtValidationError\u003e` (you\u0027d introduce `FdtValidationError::InvalidVcpufreq{Addr,Size}`)\n- `patch_vcpufreq(\u0026mut Fdt, \u0026Option\u003cVcpufreq\u003e) -\u003e libfdt::Result\u003c()\u003e` doing\n\nThen, all the `.map_err(|e| { error!(...); RebootReason::...})` are centralized in their caller, `parse_device_tree`.",
